<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kolkata</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Calcutta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kolkata (Calcutta) is the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a>. It grew from villages where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> set up a trading settlement in 1690, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> until 1911 and was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s largest cities and a major port and cultural centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolkata.jpg" alt="Kolkata" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NalGup20 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_maidan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kolkata lies on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a> in the lower Ganges Delta, at an elevation of 1.5 to 9 metres. Much of the city was originally a wetland, reclaimed over the decades to accommodate a growing population. Excavations at Chandraketugarh, 35 kilometres to the north, show that the region has been inhabited for over two millennia. The name derives from Kôlikata, the Bengali name of one of three villages that predated the British, the others being Sutanuti and Govindapur. One explanation is that it varies Kalikshetra, the area of the goddess Kali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention of the village of Kalikata is in Bipradas Pipilai's Manasa Vijay of 1495. The three villages were part of an estate belonging to the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1698. The city's recorded history began in 1690 with the arrival of the Company. Job Charnock is often regarded as its founder, but in 2003 the Calcutta High Court ruled, in response to a petition, that the city has no founder.</p><p>The Company developed the area into Fort William. Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> occupied the fort in 1756 but was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757. Under Company and then crown rule Calcutta was the de facto capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until 1911 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the second largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It was the centre of bureaucracy, politics, law, education, science and the arts, was associated with the Bengali Renaissance and was a hotbed of the Indian nationalist movement. The first newspaper in India, the Bengal Gazette, began publishing there in 1780.</p><p>The partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1947 affected the city's fortunes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> arrived in the following decades. In 1971 it hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>'s government-in-exile. Its official name, Calcutta, was changed to Kolkata in 2001.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kolkata is the capital and largest city of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, with a city population of about 4.5 million and a metropolitan area of over 15 million. Its landmarks include the Victoria Memorial and the Howrah Bridge. The University of Calcutta was the first modern university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and the Port of Kolkata is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s oldest operating port. The city's celebrations of the festival of Durga Puja are recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kolkata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English-language education, newspapers and universities founded in 1857 at <b>Calcutta</b>, Bombay and Madras created a new professional middle class.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at Masulipatnam in 1611, gained rights at Surat from the Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1612, built a factory at Madras in 1640 and leased Bombay island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at <b>Calcutta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet Rajputana was also the homeland of the Marwari merchant communities, whose firms, such as those of the Birla and Bajaj families, had become leading industrial houses in <b>Calcutta</b>, Bombay and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1786 the British judge William Jones told the Asiatic Society in <b>Calcutta</b> that Sanskrit, Greek and Latin were so alike that they must have sprung from a common source, “which, perhaps, no longer exists”: a founding moment of comparative linguistics.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gandhi took no part in the independence celebrations in Delhi: he spent 15 August 1947 in <b>Calcutta</b>, fasting, praying and spinning in a house in a riot-torn district that he shared with the Muslim League leader Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Four printed versions, all of the 19th century, became the basis for translations: <b>Calcutta</b> I, published by the British East India Company in 1814–1818; Bulaq, printed by the Egyptian government in 1835; Breslau, prepared by Christian Maximilian Habicht with Mordecai ibn al-Najjar from 1825, whose “Tunisian manuscript” was…</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
